<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arcelor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2002">2002</a>–<a href="/years/2006">2006</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arcelor was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> company formed in 2002 by the merger of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arbed">ARBED</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, Usinor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Aceralia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, with its headquarters in Luxembourg. It was the world's largest steel producer by turnover. In 2006 it was taken over by Mittal Steel after a contested bid, and the combined firm became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcelormittal">ArcelorMittal</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>In 2004 Arcelor had a turnover of €30.2 billion and shipped 45 million metric tons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a>, which made it the second largest producer by output. At its height it employed 310,000 people in more than 60 countries and supplied the automotive, construction, metal processing, household appliance and packaging industries. It made flat steel at sites such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunkirk">Dunkirk</a>, Avilés, Fos-sur-Mer, Liège and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>, as well as long steel products and stainless steel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In January 2006 Arcelor announced that it would acquire Dofasco, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>'s largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> producer, with an annual output of 4.4 million tons, after a bidding war with the German ThyssenKrupp that ended with a bid of 5.6 billion Canadian dollars. On 27 January 2006 Mittal Steel launched a hostile takeover bid for Arcelor. Arcelor's directors opposed it, as did the governments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> declared itself neutral. Indian commentators called the French opposition economic nationalism, and the Indian commerce minister Kamal Nath warned of a trade war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and France.</p><p>On 26 May 2006 Arcelor announced a merger with the steelmaker Severstal instead, a move that drew questions over its lack of transparency. On 25 June the board decided to go ahead with Mittal Steel, which had raised its offer to €40.37 a share, double the original; Arcelor paid Severstal €140 million for the failed merger. Lakshmi Mittal became president of the new company, and the Arcelor chairman Joseph Kinsch its chairman until his retirement.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arcelor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>ARBED merged into <b>Arcelor</b> in 2002, which became ArcelorMittal in 2006 with its headquarters in Luxembourg.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
